     "title": "Worked Example: Mapping the Meal-Prep Niche",
     "content": "Picture running this exercise on the meal-prep content space.\n\n**Step 1-2:** You list ten meal-prep creators and plot them on two axes: **cooking skill required (low to high)** and **audience lifestyle (general to specialized)**. Most creators cluster in the same corner — moderate cooking skill, general healthy-eating audience — with content like \"10 easy meal-prep recipes.\"\n\n**Step 3:** The empty quadrant sits at **low cooking skill, highly specialized audience**. Almost nobody is serving people who both hate cooking and have an unusual schedule constraint.\n\n**Step 4:** Running the tilt techniques against that gap produces real candidates:\n• **Sub-niche stacking**: \"15-minute meal prep for people working overnight shifts,\" narrowing both the format (15 minutes) and the audience (shift workers) at once.\n• **Persona tilt**: \"Meal prep for people who genuinely dislike cooking,\" serving a reader every other meal-prep creator implicitly assumes doesn't exist.\n• **Contrarian stance**: \"Why batch-cooking on Sunday doesn't work for most people,\" directly challenging the standard advice the entire niche repeats.\n\n**Step 5:** Testing \"meal prep for overnight shift workers\" against the two questions: could you sustain interest in this for years? Yes — the constraint itself generates endless specific content (fridge safety across shifted sleep, reheating without a full kitchen, grocery timing around irregular hours). Is the audience big enough? Shift workers are a substantial, chronically underserved population across healthcare, manufacturing, and logistics — narrow within meal-prep content, but not narrow in absolute size.",
