     "type": "application",
     "title": "Run Your Own Tilt-Mapping Session This Week",
     "content": "Block 90 minutes this week and work through the exercise on paper or a whiteboard — don't skip straight to typing, since the visual mapping is what makes the empty quadrant obvious.\n\n1. **List 8-10 real competitors** in your topic — channels, blogs, newsletters, podcasts. Include the biggest, most intimidating names, not just the small ones you feel comfortable competing with. The point is to see the whole field, not a flattering slice of it.\n\n2. **Draw a two-axis grid** using one of the pairs from this lesson (skill level, tone, audience specificity, format) and honestly plot where each competitor actually sits, not where you assume.\n\n3. **Circle the empty quadrant** and write a full sentence describing what content living there would actually look like, in specific terms, not just a label.\n\n4. **Generate three tilt candidates** from that gap using the named techniques: one sub-niche stack, one contrarian stance, one persona tilt. Write each as a one-line pitch you could say out loud to a stranger.\n\n5. **Stress-test each candidate** with the two questions — five-year sustainability and real audience size — and rank them. Commit to the top-ranked tilt for at least your next ten pieces of content before judging whether it's working, and resist the urge to switch tilts after only one or two posts.",
