     "title": "Pressure-Test Your Own Content Inc. Plan",
     "content": "Before committing years to this model, run your plan through the critiques honestly.\n\n1. **Calculate your real runway.** Add up savings, side income, and household support, and divide by your realistic monthly costs. If that number is under 18 months, either extend it or scale back your time commitment to content, rather than assuming patience alone will bridge the gap.\n\n2. **Run a pre-mortem.** Write down five specific, plausible reasons this could fail that have nothing to do with your effort or talent — a platform change, a competitor with more capital, a shift in the market. Naming them in advance makes them easier to notice early, rather than being explained away as bad luck after the fact.\n\n3. **Build a distribution hedge from day one**, using the owned-audience lesson: start the email list in month one, not month eighteen, so a platform shock doesn't erase your progress.\n\n4. **Define your explicit AI-differentiation angle**: what specifically would make a reader trust your byline over an anonymous AI-generated equivalent on the same topic? Write the answer down; if you can't, that's a gap to close now.\n\n5. **Set a real decision point**, not an open-ended commitment — for example, 'I will honestly reassess at month 18 using the Stage 1 and Stage 2 metrics from this book, not my gut feeling.'",
